-# INFORMER-LONG-SEQUENCE
+# Informer Long Sequence
+
 > **Category:** SCADA Anomaly Detection  
+> **Platform adapter:** `m06-informer-forecast`  
+> **Status:** partial (placeholder attention)  
+> **Collection position:** model **11** of 25 (alphabetical)
+
+<!-- Generated from deployment/models.catalog.json - edit the catalog, not this file. -->
+
+A long-horizon transformer forecaster for farm power, built on sparse attention that scales linearly instead of quadratically in sequence length. Its forecast residual is an anomaly signal, and its horizon is what makes day-ahead planning possible at all.
+
+## How this page fits the collection
+
+The WT-PM collection numbers its 25 repositories **alphabetically 01-25** - that is the number on this page - and gives each one a **task-based adapter id** (`m06-informer-forecast`) used by the platform registry, the `wt-pm` CLI and the fusion engine. Both are printed on every page so the ecosystem graph and the registry can be read against each other.
+
+- Repository: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-informer-long-sequence>
+- Landing page: <https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-informer-long-sequence/>
+- Ecosystem graph: <https://rajaram-2005.github.io/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/ecosystem.html>
+- Platform architecture: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md>
+- Per-model table: <https://github.com/rajaram-2005/wt-pm-lstm-scada-anomaly/blob/main/platform/docs/MODELS.md>
+
+## Model
+
+- `Informer model in model.py`
+- `ProbSparse attention block - self-declared placeholder upstream`
+
+| Block | Detail |
+|---|---|
+| Encoder | ProbSparse self-attention plus a distilling step that halves sequence length per layer |
+| Decoder | Generative-style decoder producing the whole forecast horizon in one forward pass |
+| Output | Multi-step power forecast; residual against actuals becomes the anomaly score |
+
+## Advanced concepts
+
+The ideas this model actually leans on - stated with their failure modes, not as marketing.
+
+### Sparse attention
+
+Only the queries with the most 'active' attention distributions are evaluated, reducing cost from O(L^2) to O(L log L). That is the entire reason a long-horizon transformer is deployable on 10-minute SCADA at all.
+
+### Distilling layers
+
+Each encoder layer halves the sequence, so depth buys global context instead of memory. The trade is resolution: fine structure must be captured early or it is gone.
+
+### Partial - and labelled that way
+
+Upstream ships a placeholder attention implementation. The adapter runs the real graph but the attention is not the published ProbSparse kernel, so the forecast error is honest but the architecture claim is not. Marking it partial is the point.
 
-## Description
-ProbSparse self-attention Informer architecture for ultra-long multi-step wind farm power forecasting.
+## Platform contract
 
-## Structure
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Input (adapter view) | long windows |
+| Output (`WTDataSchema`) | prediction (power forecast) + anomaly_score (forecast residual) |
+| Record format | `wt-pm.platform.v1` |
+| Typical latency | 8000 ms |
+| Deployment targets | cloud |
+| Resource requirements | torch |
+| Fallback | `m03-tcn-power-curve` |
+| Subsystem focus | whole turbine |
+| Hermes tool | `anomaly / planning context` |
+
+Records follow the shared `WTDataSchema` (`wt-pm.platform.v1`): `timestamp`, `turbine_id` and `model_id` are required; every other field is optional, so a model fills in only what it honestly produces and the fusion layer can tell absence from a zero.
+
+## Platform layers
+
+| Layer | What this model does there |
+|---|---|
+| **Fusion** | Forecast-residual anomaly voter and the long-horizon source for power-curve tracking |
+| **Hermes** | anomaly tool plus planning context for what_if |
+| **Fallback** | Falls back to m03-tcn-power-curve when torch is unavailable |
+| **Evaluation** | Scored on both forecast error and residual-based detection - a good forecaster can be a poor detector |
+
+Layers are described once, in the catalog, and referenced by every model that participates in them: fusion, calibrated uncertainty, drift and data trust, explainability, physics constraints, the safety gate, the evaluation protocol and the Hermes agent.
+
